    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_a48e61300755ee96ced938cf6232ff87\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"[2409.08792] Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"archive\\nSearch Submit Donate Log in\\nComputer Science > Computation and Language\\narXiv:2409.08792 (cs)\\n[Submitted on 13 Sep 2024]\\nTitle: Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\\nAuthors: Luis Rita , Josh Southern , Ivan Laponogov , Kyle Higgins , Kirill Veselkov\\nView PDF\\nAbstract: In the emerging field of computational gastronomy, aligning culinary practices with scientifically supported nutritional goals is increasingly important.\\nThis study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content of meals. Phytochemicals are bioactive compounds found in plants, which, based on preclinical studies, may offer potential health benefits.\\nWe fine-tuned models, including OpenAI's GPT-3.5, DaVinci, and Meta's TinyLlama, using an ingredient substitution dataset. These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance phytochemical content and create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nOur approach improved Hit@1 accuracy on ingredient substitution tasks, from the baseline 34.53 plus-minus 0.10% to 38.03 plus-minus 0.28% on the original GISMo dataset, and from 40.24 plus-minus 0.36% to 54.46 plus-minus 0.29% on a refined version of the same dataset.\\nThese substitutions led to the creation of 1,951 phytochemically enriched ingredient pairings and 1,639 unique recipes. While this approach demonstrates potential in optimizing ingredient substitutions, caution must be taken when drawing conclusions about health benefits, as the claims are based on preclinical evidence.\\nFuture work should include clinical validation and broader datasets to further evaluate the nutritional impact of these substitutions. This research represents a step forward in using AI to promote healthier eating practices, providing potential pathways for integrating computational methods with nutritional science.\\nFrom: Kirill Veselkov Dr [ view email ] [v1] Fri, 13 Sep 2024 12:55:45 UTC (2,324 KB)\\nFull-text links:\\nAccess Paper:\\n* View PDF\\n  license icon view license\\nCurrent browse context:\\ncs.CL\\n< prev | next >\\nnew | recent | 2024-09\\nChange to browse by:\\ncs\\nReferences & Citations\\n* NASA ADS\\n* Google Scholar\\n* Semantic Scholar\\n  export BibTeX citation\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://profiles.imperial.ac.uk/l.rita19/publications\",\n      \"title\": \"Luis Rita | Publications | Imperial College London\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"...7 more\\nDOI DOI: 10.1038/s41598-026-59617-1 (opens in a new tab) ⁠\\nPDF DOWNLOAD PDF (opens in a new tab) MORE VIEW MORE INFO (opens in a new tab)\\n* JOURNAL ARTICLE\\nOptimizing ingredient substitution using large language models to enhance phytochemical content in recipes (opens in a new tab) ⁠\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\\nThis study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content of meals. Phytochemicals are bioactive compounds found in plants, which, based on preclinical studies, may offer potential health benefits.\\nWe fine-tuned models, including OpenAI’s GPT-3.5, DaVinci, and Meta’s TinyLlama, using an ingredient substitution dataset. These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance phytochemical content and create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nThese substitutions led to the creation of 1,951 phytochemically enriched ingredient pairings and 1,639 unique recipes. While this approach demonstrates potential in optimizing ingredient substitutions, caution must be taken when drawing conclusions about health benefits, as the claims are based on preclinical evidence.\\nIt illustrates the creation of new culinary recipes with the highest number of phytochemically enriched ingredients. The process from model selection, recipe generation, to the final selection based on phytochemical content is demonstrated.\\n3. Results\\nThis process resulted in the generation of 1,951 phytochemically enriched ingredient\\nsubstitutions, leading to 1,639 unique recipes, each featuring at least one phytochemically rich ingredient.\\nRita L, Neumann NR, Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Veselkov D, Pratico D, et al. Alzheimer’s disease: using gene/protein network machine learning for molecule discovery in olive oil. Hum Genomics. 2023;17:57.\\n31. Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Shepherd M, Qureshi A, Veselkov D, Charkoftaki G, et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2504-4990/6/4/131\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nby L Rita · 2024 · Cited by 8 — This study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nThis study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content of meals.\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to ...\\nThis study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nBy fine-tuning these models on a dataset of recipes and ingredient substitutes, we aim to develop an algorithm that not only understands the interplay of flavors and nutritional aspects in cooking but also tailors suggestions to the preferences and requirements of each user.\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nWe fine-tuned models, including OpenAI’s GPT-3.5-Turbo, DaVinci-002, and Meta’s TinyLlama-1.1B, using an ingredient substitution dataset. These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance the phytochemical content and to create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nby L Rita · 2024 · Cited by 13 — This study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nby L Rita · 2024 · Cited by 11 — This study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nby L Rita · 2024 · Cited by 13 — This study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to ...\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Artificial-Intelligence-Enabled-Ingredient-in-Food-Oz-Oz/6421bd69a910663a6bbfd0dcff6dceb5773565d3\",\n      \"title\": \"[PDF] Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Ingredient Substitution ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"[PDF] Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Ingredient Substitution ...\\nThis study explores how large language models can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content of meals, and improves the top ingredient prediction accuracy on substitution tasks.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386159709_Optimizing_Ingredient_Substitution_Using_Large_Language_Models_to_Enhance_Phytochemical_Content_in_Recipes/fulltext/6746fca1790d154bf9aed4f4/Optimizing-Ingredient-Substitution-Using-Large-Language-Models-to-Enhance-Phytochemical-Content-in-Recipes.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nThis research represents a step forward in using AI to promote healthier eating practices, providing potential pathways for integrating computational methods with nutritional science.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Large-Language-Models-Evaluation-for-Role-Aware-in-Alj-Mourhir/cc27190337d6ecaefed6cd534afe21c918420f70\",\n      \"title\": \"Large Language Models Evaluation for Role-Aware ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Large Language Models Evaluation for Role-Aware ...\\nThis study explores how large language models can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.04922v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"In this paper, we address the challenge of recipe personalization through ingredient substitution. We make use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to build an ingredient substitution system designed to predict plausible substitute ingredients within a given recipe context.\\nLarge Language Models (LLMs), combined with advanced prompting techniques, have emerged as the current de-facto solution for NLP tasks in various domains. However, their application in ingredient substitution remains under-explored, and we are aware of only one very recent work Rita et al. (2024) .\\nTherefore, this paper aims to implement an LLM-based ingredient substitution solution that considers the recipe’s context for accurate ingredient replacement.\\n(2023) Fatemi, B., Duval, Q., Girdhar, R., Drozdzal, M., Romero-Soriano, A.: Learning to substitute ingredients in recipes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.07960 (2023)\\n* Rita et al.\\n(2024) Rita, L., Southern, J., Laponogov, I., Higgins, K., Veselkov, K.: Optimizing ingredient substitution using large language models to enhance phytochemical content in recipes. Machine Learning and Knowledge Extraction 6 (4), 2738–2752 (2024)\\n* Boscarino et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.04922\",\n      \"title\": \"Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-12-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\\nrishemjit.kaur@csio.res.in;\\nAbstract\\nIn this paper, we address the challenge of recipe personalization through ingre- dient substitution. We make use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to build an ingredient substitution system designed to predict plausible substitute ingredi- ents within a given recipe context.\\nParallel to our work, Rita et al. [6] experimented with LLMs for the ingredient substitution task. However, they have only employed SFT, and there has been no study on identifying the most effective prompt pattern or the recipe context.\\n3\\n2.2 Large Language Models\\nIntroduction\\n[5] Fatemi, B., Duval, Q., Girdhar, R., Drozdzal, M., Romero-Soriano, A.: Learning to substitute ingredients in recipes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.07960 (2023)\\n[6] Rita, L., Southern, J., Laponogov, I., Higgins, K., Veselkov, K.: Optimizing ingre- dient substitution using large language models to enhance phytochemical content in recipes.\\n[21] White, J., Fu, Q., Hays, S., Sandborn, M., Olea, C., Gilbert, H., Elnashar, A., Spencer-Smith, J., Schmidt, D.C.: A Prompt Pattern Catalog to Enhance Prompt Engineering with ChatGPT (2023)\\n[22] Hu, E.J., Wallis, P., Allen-Zhu, Z., Li, Y., Wang, S., Wang, L., Chen, W., et al. : Lora: Low-rank adaptation of large language models.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2949719125000536\",\n      \"title\": \"Large language models for ingredient substitution in food ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Large language models for ingredient substitution in food ...\\nby T Senath · 2025 · Cited by 14 — Extensive experiments to determine the best LLM as well as optimization strategies for the ingredient substitution task. •. Presents a system that\\nLarge language models for ingredient substitution in food ...\\nby T Senath · 2025 · Cited by 12 — Optimizing the results of the fine-tuned model. Different optimization techniques are applied on the fine-tuned LLM, namely two stage fine-tuning, multi-task\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_eb4212b0affedKnHljTtyGboxL\"\n}"
