    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_89f97f8853dba395205434af86f6a16a\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - facebookresearch/gismo: companion code for \\\"Learning to substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\\" · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Description: companion code for \\\"Learning to substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\\" - facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Stars: 28\\n* Forks: 5\\n* Default branch: main\\n* Created: 2023-02-07T18:52:54.000Z\\n* Commits: 2\\nTop-level files\\nInverseCooking 2.0: Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module (GISMo)\\nCode to reproduce the experiments of Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes .\\nInstallation\\nTraining ingredient substitution module\\nPlease follow the instructions here .\\nTraining image-to-recipe\\nRunning experiments\\nGISMo model allows to provide substitution suggestion. For each of the validation inputs, this model will write potential substitutions. To evaluate the impact of substitutions generated by GISMo on the image-to-recipe pipeline, you can perform the following test.\\nPre-trained models\\n* Inverse Cooking: model , ingredients vocabulary , recipes vocabulary , titles vocabulary\\n* GisMO: model\\nContributing\\nSee CONTRIBUTING.md .\\nDeveloper tools\\nThe following commands should be used prior to submitting a fix:\\nThe majority of ingredient-substitution is licensed under CC-BY-NC, see LICENSE for details.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2302.07960v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes - arXiv.org\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Refer to caption Figure 1 : Overview of personalized image-to-recipe generation system with ingredient substitution. Given an ingredient to substitute, our ingredient substitution model, GISMo, suggests an alternative ingredient. The set of ingredients is updated and then used together with the image to generate the personalized recipe.\\nMoreover, we introduce G raph-based I ngredient S ubstitution Mo dule (GISMo), a novel ingredient substitution model which leverages not only the substitution context from a recipe but also generic ingredient relational information found in a large corpus of recipes, available through the FlavorGraph.\\n* •\\nWe create Recipe1MSubs, a benchmark for ingredient substitution.\\n* •\\nWe develop GISMo, a novel GNN-based ingredient substitution model, which leverages both recipe-specific context and generic ingredient relations to predict and rank plausible substitutes and improves upon previous approaches by a large margin.\\n* •\\nFigure 2 : Overview of GISMo, which is composed of: 1) ingredient encoder, 2) context encoder, and 3) ingredient substitution decoder. The input graph models generic ingredient and food molecule-ingredient relations.\\nWe use the Adam [ 30 ] optimizer, and perform early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set in all cases. We defer more details to the supplementary material.\\nPersonalized inverse cooking pipeline details. We train our pipeline following [ 49 ] .\\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [ 44 ] , used deep graph library (DGL) [ 57 ] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [ 30 ] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of $1000$ .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes G X\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-02-17\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We use the Adam [30] optimizer, and perform early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set in all cases. We defer more details to the supplementary material. Personalized inverse cooking pipeline details. We train our pipeline following [49].\\nA. GISMo Implementation Details\\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali- dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 1000.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2504-4990/6/4/131\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nBy fine-tuning these models on a dataset of recipes and ingredient substitutes, we aim to develop an algorithm that not only understands the interplay of flavors and nutritional aspects in cooking but also tailors suggestions to the preferences and requirements of each user.\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language ...\\nWe fine-tuned models, including OpenAI’s GPT-3.5-Turbo, DaVinci-002, and Meta’s TinyLlama-1.1B, using an ingredient substitution dataset. These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance the phytochemical content and to create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to  Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes | alphaXiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Abstract Paper\\nSubmitted 13 Sept 2024\\nOptimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\\nImperial College London Boston Children’s Hospital Harvard Medical School\\nYale University\\nLR Luis Rita JS Josh Southern IL Ivan Laponogov Kyle Higgins Kyle Higgins KV Kirill Veselkov +1 more Show less\\nIn the emerging field of computational gastronomy, aligning culinary practices with scientifically supported nutritional goals is increasingly important. This study explores how large language models (LLMs) can be applied to optimize ingredient substitutions in recipes, specifically to enhance the phytochemical content of meals.\\nPhytochemicals are bioactive compounds found in plants, which, based on preclinical studies, may offer potential health benefits. We fine-tuned models, including OpenAI's GPT-3.5, DaVinci, and Meta's TinyLlama, using an ingredient substitution dataset.\\nThese models were used to predict substitutions that enhance phytochemical content and create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nOur approach improved Hit@1 accuracy on ingredient substitution tasks, from the baseline 34.53 plus-minus 0.10% to 38.03 plus-minus 0.28% on the original GISMo dataset, and from 40.24 plus-minus 0.36% to 54.46 plus-minus 0.29% on a refined version of the same dataset.\\nThese substitutions led to the creation of 1,951 phytochemically enriched ingredient pairings and 1,639 unique recipes. While this approach demonstrates potential in optimizing ingredient substitutions, caution must be taken when drawing conclusions about health benefits, as the claims are based on preclinical evidence.\\n@misc {rita2024optimizingingredientsubstitutionusing, title ={ Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes }, author ={ Luis Rita and Josh Southern and Ivan Laponogov and Kyle Higgins and Kirill Veselkov }, year ={ 2024 }, eprint ={ 2409.08792 }, archivePrefix ={ arXiv }, primaryClass\\n={ cs.CL }, url ={ https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08792 }, }\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/activecm/rita/releases\",\n      \"title\": \"Releases · activecm/rita · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Skip to content ## Navigation Menu\\n1. activecm\\n2. rita\\nRepository navigation\\n* Code\\n* Issues 15 (15)\\n* Pull requests 10 (10)\\n* Actions\\n* Projects\\n* Security and quality\\n* Insights\\n  More items # Releases: activecm/rita\\n  Releases Tags\\nRelease list\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/jzhang38/tinyllama\",\n      \"title\": \"The TinyLlama project is an open endeavor to pretrain a ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The TinyLlama project is an open endeavor to pretrain a ...\\nThose optimizations also greatly reduce the memory footprint, allowing us to stuff our 1.1B model into 40GB GPU RAM and train with a per-gpu batch size of 16k\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2401.02385\",\n      \"title\": \"TinyLlama: An Open-Source Small Language Model - arXiv.org\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"TinyLlama: An Open-Source Small Language Model - arXiv.org\\nIn this paper, we introduce TinyLlama, an open-source, small-scale language model. With its compact architecture and promising performance, TinyLlama can enable end-user applications on mobile devices, and serve as a lightweight platform for testing a wide range of innovative ideas related to language models.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/jzhang38/TinyLlama/blob/main/speculative_decoding/README.md\",\n      \"title\": \"TinyLlama/speculative_decoding/README.md at main - GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"TinyLlama/speculative_decoding/README.md at main - GitHub\\nWe use PY007/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v0.1 as the assistant model and vary the large model from guanaco-7B to 33B. Experiments are done on a single A40 GPU with code inside instruct_hf_assisted_decoding.py. TinyLlama is loaded in fp16 and the large models are loaded in 8 bit to make guanaco-33b fit in memory and also to keep a consistent setup.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://huggingface.co/TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0\",\n      \"title\": \"TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 - Hugging Face\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0 - Hugging Face\\nInput a message to start chatting with TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0. We’re on a journey to advance and democratize artificial intelligence through open source and open science.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_eb4212b0affedKnHljTtyGboxL\"\n}"
