    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_40d351e520ee69d5f7aebfa284ceb731\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"This benchmark setup includes a learning rate of 5e-05, weight decay of 0.0001, 300 hidden units per layer, an embedding dimension of 300, and a dropout rate of 0.25, across two layers, and involves 400 training rounds with regular negative sampling and random embedding initialization.\\nThe model optimisation was conducted using paged_adamw_32bit with a learning rate set at 5e-4, and a cosine learning rate scheduler was employed for optimal learning rate adjustments throughout the training phase. A\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes G X\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-02-17\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We tuned the learning rate lr using the set { 0 . 005 , 0 . 001 , 0 . 0005 , 0 . 0001 , 0 . 00005 , 0 . 00001 , 0 . 000005 } ,\\nand both the number of features F and ingredient embed- ding dimension d using the set { 200 , 300 , 400 , 500 , 600 } .\\nOur final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi- tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi- ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300.\\nThe model is trained with a learning rate lr = 0 . 00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0 . 0001 and a dropout rate of 0 . 25.\\nB. Visual inspection of recipes\\nAll ingredient predictions and recipe generations visual- ized in the figures are based on our best ViT-16 MLS.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2302.07960\",\n      \"title\": \"[2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We tuned the learning rate $lr$ using the set ${0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005}$ , and both the number of features $F$ and ingredient embedding dimension $d$ using the set ${200,300,400,500,600}$ .\\nOur final model consists of an ingredient encoder ( $\\\\mathrm{IE}$ ) composed of two GIN gin layers, and an ingredient substitution decoder ( $\\\\mathrm{ISD}$ ) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses $F=300$ . The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to $d=300$ .\\nThe model is trained with a learning rate $lr=0.00005$ , and is regularized using a weight decay of $0.0001$ and a dropout rate of $0.25$ .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2302.07960v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes - arXiv.org\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"We tuned the learning rate $lr$ using the set ${0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005}$ , and both the number of features $F$ and ingredient embedding dimension $d$ using the set ${200,300,400,500,600}$ .\\nThe input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to $d=300$ . The model is trained with a learning rate $lr=0.00005$ , and is regularized using a weight decay of $0.0001$ and a dropout rate of $0.25$ .\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2949719125000536\",\n      \"title\": \"Large language models for ingredient substitution in food ...\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Large language models for ingredient substitution in food ...\\nby T Senath · 2025 · Cited by 19 — These learned embeddings were applied to the context of ingredient substitution in dietary use cases. ... Exploiting Food Embeddings for Ingredient Substitution.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://researchr.org/publication/SenathACRK25\",\n      \"title\": \"Large language models for ingredient substitution in food recipes using supervised fine-tuning and direct preference optimization - researchr publication \",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"# Large language models for ingredient substitution in food recipes using supervised fine-tuning and direct preference optimization Thevin Senath , Kumuthu Athukorala , Ransika Costa , Surangika Ranathunga , Rishemjit Kaur .\\nLarge language models for ingredient substitution in food recipes using supervised fine-tuning and direct preference optimization . Nat. Lang. Process. J. , 12: 100177 , 2025. [doi] * Abstract\\n* Authors\\n* BibTeX\\n* References\\n* Bibliographies\\n* Reviews\\n* Related\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.04922\",\n      \"title\": \"[2412.04922] Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-12-06\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"archive\\nSearch Submit Donate Log in\\nComputer Science > Computation and Language\\narXiv:2412.04922 (cs)\\n[Submitted on 6 Dec 2024]\\nTitle: Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\\nAuthors: Thevin Senath , Kumuthu Athukorala , Ransika Costa , Surangika Ranathunga , Rishemjit Kaur\\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\\nAbstract: In this paper, we address the challenge of recipe personalization through ingredient substitution.\\nWe make use of Large Language Models (LLMs) to build an ingredient substitution system designed to predict plausible substitute ingredients within a given recipe context. Given that the use of LLMs for this task has been barely done, we carry out an extensive set of experiments to determine the best LLM, prompt, and the fine-tuning setups.\\nWe further experiment with methods such as multi-task learning, two-stage fine-tuning, and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO). The experiments are conducted using the publicly available Recipe1MSub corpus. The best results are produced by the Mistral7-Base LLM after fine-tuning and DPO.\\nThis result outperforms the strong baseline available for the same corpus with a Hit@1 score of 22.04. Thus we believe that this research represents a significant step towards enabling personalized and creative culinary experiences by utilizing LLM-based ingredient substitution.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://portal.fis.tum.de/de/publications/exploiting-food-embeddings-for-ingredient-substitution\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n        Exploiting food embeddings for ingredient substitution\\n      -  Technische Universität München\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Zur Hauptnavigation wechseln Zur Suche wechseln Zum Hauptinhalt wechseln\\nExploiting food embeddings for ingredient substitution\\n* Chantal Pellegrini\\n* , Ege Özsoy\\n* , Monika Wintergerst\\n* , Georg Groh\\n* Informatik 25 - Lehrstuhl für Data Science and Engineering\\n* Technische Universität München\\n  Publikation : Beitrag in Buch/Bericht/Konferenzband › Konferenzbeitrag › Begutachtung\\n  27 Zitate (Scopus)\\n* Übersicht\\n* Fingerprint\\nAbstract\\nIn this paper, we present two models for ingredient embeddings, Food2Vec and FoodBERT. In addition, we combine both approaches with images, resulting in two multimodal representation models. FoodBERT is furthermore used for relation extraction. We conduct a ground truth based evaluation for all approaches, as well as a human evaluation.\\nUntersuchen Sie die Forschungsthemen von „Exploiting food embeddings for ingredient substitution“. Zusammen bilden sie einen einzigartigen Fingerprint.\\n* APA\\n* Author\\n* BIBTEX\\n* Harvard\\n* Standard\\n* RIS\\n* Vancouver\\nPellegrini, C., Özsoy, E., Wintergerst, M. , & Groh, G. (2021). Exploiting food embeddings for ingredient substitution . in C. Pesquita, A. Fred, & H.\\n(HEALTHINF 2021 - 14th International Conference on Health Informatics; Part of the 14th International Joint Conference on Biomedical Engineering Systems and Technologies, BIOSTEC 2021). SciTePress.\\nPellegrini, Chantal ; Özsoy, Ege ; Wintergerst, Monika et al. / Exploiting food embeddings for ingredient substitution .\\nIn this paper, we present two models for ingredient embeddings, Food2Vec and FoodBERT. In addition, we combine both approaches with images, resulting in two multimodal representation models. FoodBERT is furthermore used for relation extraction. We conduct a ground truth based evaluation for all approaches, as well as a human evaluation.\\nIn this paper, we present two models for ingredient embeddings, Food2Vec and FoodBERT. In addition, we combine both approaches with images, resulting in two multimodal representation models. FoodBERT is furthermore used for relation extraction. We conduct a ground truth based evaluation for all approaches, as well as a human evaluation.\\nOn top of that, a lack of standardized evaluation metrics makes comparing approaches difficult. In this paper, we present two models for ingredient embeddings, Food2Vec and FoodBERT. In addition, we combine both approaches with images, resulting in two multimodal representation models. FoodBERT is furthermore used for relation extraction.\\nWe conduct a ground truth based evaluation for all approaches, as well as a human evaluation. The comparison shows that FoodBERT, and especially the multimodal version, is best suited for substitute recommendations in dietary use cases.\\nKW - BERT\\nKW - Food Substitution\\nKW - Word Embeddings\\nKW - Word2Vec\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution: Official repository of the paper \\\"Exploiting Food Embeddings for Ingredient Substitution\\\". · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\\n* Description: Official repository of the paper \\\"Exploiting Food Embeddings for Ingredient Substitution\\\". - ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\\n* Stars: 24\\n* Forks: 7\\n* Default branch: master\\nTop-level files\\n* comment_scraping/\\n* data/\\n* evaluation/\\n* food2vec/\\n* foodbert/\\n* foodbert_embeddings/\\n* multimodal/\\n* normalisation/\\n* relation_extraction/\\n* .gitignore\\n* README.md\\n* requirements.txt\\nREADME.md\\nFoodBERT: Exploiting Food Embeddings for Ingredient Substitution\\nOfficial repository of the paper \\\"Exploiting Food Embeddings for Ingredient Substitution\\\" ( Published at the International Conference on Health Informatics 2021 ).\\nIdentifying fitting substitutes for cooking ingredients can be beneficial for various goals, such as nutrient optimization, avoiding allergens, or adapting a recipe to personal preferences. In this repository, we present two models for ingredient embeddings, Food2Vec and FoodBERT.\\n* Download https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution/releases/download/0.1/food2vec_models.zip and place the content in ./food2vec/models\\n* Download https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution/releases/download/0.1/foodbert_data.zip and place the content in ./foodbert/data\\n* Download https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution/releases/download/0.1/foodbert_embeddings_data.zip and place the content in ./foodbert_embeddings/data\\n* Download https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution/releases/download/0.1/multimodal_data.zip and place the content in ./multimodal/data\\n* Download https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution/releases/download/0.1/relation_extraction_models.zip and place the content in ./relation_extraction/models\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution: Stop replacing salt with sugar!: Towards Intuitive Human-Agent Teaching\\\",  Nikolaos Kondylidis, Andrea Rafanelli, Ilaria Tiddi, Annette Ten Teije, Frank Van Harmelen,  Presented at the 22nd European Conference on Multi-Agent Systems (EUMAS 2025), Bucharest · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\\n* Description: Stop replacing salt with sugar!: Towards Intuitive Human-Agent Teaching\\\",  Nikolaos Kondylidis, Andrea Rafanelli, Ilaria Tiddi, Annette Ten Teije, Frank Van Harmelen,  Presented at the 22nd Eu...\\n* Stars: 4\\n* Forks: 1\\nP_Networks | FoodBert\\npython main.py experiment.num_repetitions=4 ingredients.vocabulary=foodbert teacher.active_learning_method=Random student.learning_method=P_Networks student.input_representation.ingredient_representation=foodbert_embed\\n2.2 Best performing Learning Methods, for different Tutoring Policies\\nBalanced | Accumulative | 1-hot & FoodOn\\nNote: Some code is adapted from: https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_eb4212b0affedKnHljTtyGboxL\"\n}"
