    "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9c475ec0b93f472e",
    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_778597e1036395f2598729847f51a87c\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - facebookresearch/gismo: companion code for \\\"Learning to substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\\" · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Description: companion code for \\\"Learning to substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\\" - facebookresearch/gismo\\n* Stars: 28\\n* Forks: 5\\n* Default branch: main\\n* Created: 2023-02-07T18:52:54.000Z\\n* Commits: 2\\nTop-level files\\nInverseCooking 2.0: Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module (GISMo)\\nCode to reproduce the experiments of Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes .\\nInstallation\\nTraining ingredient substitution module\\nPlease follow the instructions here .\\nTraining image-to-recipe\\nRunning experiments\\nGISMo model allows to provide substitution suggestion. For each of the validation inputs, this model will write potential substitutions. To evaluate the impact of substitutions generated by GISMo on the image-to-recipe pipeline, you can perform the following test.\\nThe majority of ingredient-substitution is licensed under CC-BY-NC, see LICENSE for details.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2409.08792\",\n      \"title\": \"Optimizing Ingredient Substitution Using Large Language Models to Enhance Phytochemical Content in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-09-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Luís Rita 1 , Josh Southern 2 , Ivan Laponogov 1 , Kyle Higgins 1,3 and Kirill Veselkov 1,4*\\n1 Division of Cancer, Department of Surgery and Cancer, Faculty of Medicine, Imperial College London, London, UK\\n2 Department of Computing, Faculty of Engineering, Imperial College London, London, UK\\nWe fine-tuned models, including OpenAI’s GPT-3.5, DaVinci, and Meta’s TinyLlama, using an ingredient substitution dataset. These models were used to predict substitutions that enhance phytochemical content and create a corresponding enriched recipe dataset.\\nData Availability Statement: Fine-tuned models GISMo and TinyLlama, along with the scripts used for filtering the Recipe1MSubs dataset and fine-tuning the GISMo, DaVinci, GPT-3.5, and TinyLlama models, are hosted on the Bitbucket repository.\\nAdditionally, data on the categories each ingredient from Recipe1M belongs to, as well as the script used to retrieve these categories, are also made available. The repository can be accessed at the following URL: https://bitbucket.org/iAnalytica/recipe-generator.\\nRita L, Neumann NR, Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Veselkov D, Pratico D, et al. Alzheimer’s disease: using gene/protein network machine learning for molecule discovery in olive oil. Hum Genomics. 2023;17:57.\\n31. Laponogov I, Gonzalez G, Shepherd M, Qureshi A, Veselkov D, Charkoftaki G, et al.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/cqx931/RiTa\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - cqx931/RiTa: RiTa: the generative language toolkit · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"cqx931/RiTa\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/cqx931/RiTa\\n* Description: RiTa: the generative language toolkit\\n* Stars: 0\\n* Forks: 0\\n* License: GPL-3.0 license\\n* Default branch: master\\n* Created: 2015-06-28T16:37:03.000Z\\n* Commits: 1,538\\nTop-level files\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.04922\",\n      \"title\": \"Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-12-09\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Large Language Models for Ingredient Substitution in Food Recipes using Supervised Fine-tuning and Direct Preference Optimization\\n[5]’s pioneering work Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module (GISMo) uses recipe-specific context and generic ingredient relational information encoded as a graph simultaneously to predict suitable substitutions.\\nWe carry out further experiments using two-stage fine-tuning, multi-task fine-tuning, as well as Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) techniques. Our best model that employs both SFT and DPO outperforms the strong baseline of GISMO, with a Hit@1 score of 22.04. To summarize, the contributions of this paper are as follows:\\n2\\nGISMo uses recent advancements in graph neural networks (GNNs) to capture interactions between ingredients within learned ingredient embeddings. These embed- dings are then refined with contextual information from a given recipe, enabling the prediction of plausible ingredient substitutions.\\nParallel to our work, Rita et al. [6] experimented with LLMs for the ingredient substitution task. However, they have only employed SFT, and there has been no study on identifying the most effective prompt pattern or the recipe context.\\n3\\n2.2 Large Language Models\\nIntroduction\\nAfterwards, the resulting model is further fine-tuned with data for the ingredient substitution task, resulting in two-stage fine-tuning.\\nFig. 3 The prompt used to fine-tune the LLM with question-answer pairs on the ingredients needed to prepare a dish\\n[INST] What are the ingredients we need to make Cool ’n Easy Creamy Watermelon Pie?\\nIn this approach, the model is fine-tuned with both ingredient substitution task, as well as the new task we introduced in the previous section.\\nDirect Preference Optimization (DPO)\\nTo implement DPO, we construct a preference dataset from training data that initially yielded undesirable or incorrect responses to ingredient substitution prompts.\\nFollowing DPO, we further fine-tune the optimized model again for the ingredient substitution task. As further discussed in Section 5, two-stage fine-tuning and multi-task fine-tuning did not result in any gains. Consequently, the model that employs SFT and DPO emerged as the best solution.\\nSince we use the same dataset with the same data splits as GISMO, we can directly compare our results with GISMO. Note that we cannot use Rita et al. [6]’s work as a baseline, because most of their gains appear to result from a data preprocessing step in which ingredients from the same category are clustered together.\\n[5] Fatemi, B., Duval, Q., Girdhar, R., Drozdzal, M., Romero-Soriano, A.: Learning to substitute ingredients in recipes. arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.07960 (2023)\\n[6] Rita, L., Southern, J., Laponogov, I., Higgins, K., Veselkov, K.: Optimizing ingre- dient substitution using large language models to enhance phytochemical content in recipes.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://cris.maastrichtuniversity.nl/en/publications/enhancing-gismo-integrating-recipe-contexts-into-a-graph-based-in\",\n      \"title\": \"\\n        Enhancing GISMo: Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module\\n      -  Maastricht University\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"To comply with dietary restrictions, individuals seek to replace ingredients in culinary recipes. The advanced model Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module, called GISMo, recently was proposed to facilitate the learning of ingredient substitutions within recipes.\\nWe identify some potential improvements in GISMo’s functionality for generating substitution recommendations based on recipe contexts by capturing various graph representations of recipe contexts, including ingredient-recipe relationships and cooking actions.\\nKeywords\\n* graph neural network\\n* ingredient substitution\\n* knowledge graph\\n* recipe context\\nFingerprint\\nDive into the research topics of 'Enhancing GISMo: Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\\n/ Enhancing GISMo : Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module . In: CEUR Workshop Proceedings . 2025 ; Vol. 4196. pp. 43-53.\\n@article{86114ed0ad5d4a8ca8c99263c19672cc,\\ntitle = \\\"Enhancing GISMo: Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module\\\",\\nabstract = \\\"To comply with dietary restrictions, individuals seek to replace ingredients in culinary recipes. The advanced model Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module, called GISMo, recently was proposed to facilitate the learning of ingredient substitutions within recipes.\\nOur findings demonstrate that the proposed changes in GISMo can augment the diversity of food substitution recommendations without compromising prediction quality.\\\",\\nkeywords = \\\"graph neural network, ingredient substitution, knowledge graph, recipe context\\\",\\nLoesch, J , Schimmel, D, Erdem, F, Kalkavan, D, Bilgin, Z & Celebi, R 2025, ' Enhancing GISMo: Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module ', CEUR Workshop Proceedings , vol. 4196, pp. 43-53.\\nEnhancing GISMo: Integrating Recipe Contexts into a Graph-Based Ingredient Substitution Module.\\nN2 - To comply with dietary restrictions, individuals seek to replace ingredients in culinary recipes. The advanced model Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module, called GISMo, recently was proposed to facilitate the learning of ingredient substitutions within recipes.\\nWe identify some potential improvements in GISMo’s functionality for generating substitution recommendations based on recipe contexts by capturing various graph representations of recipe contexts, including ingredient-recipe relationships and cooking actions.\\nThe advanced model Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module, called GISMo, recently was proposed to facilitate the learning of ingredient substitutions within recipes.\\nWe identify some potential improvements in GISMo’s functionality for generating substitution recommendations based on recipe contexts by capturing various graph representations of recipe contexts, including ingredient-recipe relationships and cooking actions.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution: Stop replacing salt with sugar!: Towards Intuitive Human-Agent Teaching\\\",  Nikolaos Kondylidis, Andrea Rafanelli, Ilaria Tiddi, Annette Ten Teije, Frank Van Harmelen,  Presented at the 22nd European Conference on Multi-Agent Systems (EUMAS 2025), Bucharest · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/human-agent-teaming/efficient-ingredient-substitution\\n* Description: Stop replacing salt with sugar!: Towards Intuitive Human-Agent Teaching\\\",  Nikolaos Kondylidis, Andrea Rafanelli, Ilaria Tiddi, Annette Ten Teije, Frank Van Harmelen,  Presented at the 22nd Eu...\\n* Stars: 4\\n* Forks: 1\\nStop replacing salt with sugar!\\nThis repository contains the code and instructions for reproducing the experiments presented in:\\nBalanced | Accumulative | 1-hot & FoodOn\\n1. Follow the instructions of the GISMo repository\\n2. Download their vocabulary file\\nwget https://dl.fbaipublicfiles.com/gismo/vocab_ingrs.pkl\\nmv vocab_ingrs.pkl Recipe1MSubs_data/vocab_ingrs.pkl\\n3.\\nBalanced | Accumulative | 1-hot & FoodOn\\nExtract the GISMo Vocabulary and create a new ingredient ID catalogue, together with all ingredient ID <-> name mappings\\npython create_Recipe1MSubs_and_FlavorGraph_ingredient_mappings.py\\nBalanced | Accumulative | 1-hot & FoodOn\\nNote: Some code is adapted from: https://github.com/ChantalMP/Exploiting-Food-Embeddings-for-Ingredient-Substitution\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/cliao5/GISMO\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - cliao5/GISMO · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Navigation Menu\\nToggle navigation\\nSign in\\nAppearance settings\\nSearch or jump to...\\nSearch code, repositories, users, issues, pull requests...\\nSign in\\nSign up\\nAppearance settings\\nResetting focus\\ncliao5 / GISMO Public\\n17 Commits\\n* 17 Commits\\n* GISMO-mis | GISMO-mis |  |\\n* GenerateScore | GenerateScore |  |\\n* one2_matrices | one2_matrices |  |\\n* partitioned-heritability | partitioned-heritability |  |\\n* .gitignore | .gitignore |  |\\n* README.md | README.md |  |\\n* View all files\\nRepository files navigation\\n* README\\n  More items\\nGISMO\\n* If there is a deletion (\\\"-\\\") anywhere in either reference or query sequence for a gene, skip over it (not counted towards mis or syn totals)\\n* For any \\\"N\\\" bp, consider all possible substitutions. To be the most conservative, if it can be synonymous, mark is as synonymous.\\n* Generating GISMO one2 matrix (primary output: GenerateScore/inputs/unmerged-species_combined_matrix_2023-07-26.tsv )\\n* Input files hosted at: https://genome.senckenberg.de/download/TOGA/human_hg38_reference/\\npartitioned-heritability/\\n* Scripts to run partitioned heritability\\ninputs/\\noutputs/\\n* Partitioned heritability outputs for both all- and psych- traits\\nscripts/\\n* Scripts used to run partitioned heritability\\nAbout\\nNo description, website, or topics provided.\\nResources\\nReadme\\nActivity\\nStars\\n1 star\\nWatchers\\n1 watching\\nForks\\n0 forks\\nReport repository\\nReleases 1\\nGISMO v1.0.0 Latest Jun 4, 2026\\nPackages\\nContributors\\nLanguages\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2023arXiv230207960F/abstract\",\n      \"title\": \"Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes - NASA/ADS\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-02-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"To address ingredient substitution, we build a benchmark, composed of a dataset of substitution pairs with standardized splits, evaluation metrics, and baselines.\\nWe further introduce Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module (GISMo), a novel model that leverages the context of a recipe as well as generic ingredient relational information encoded within a graph to rank plausible substitutions.\\nWe show through comprehensive experimental validation that GISMo surpasses the best performing baseline by a large margin in terms of mean reciprocal rank. Finally, we highlight the benefits of GISMo by integrating it in an improved image-to-recipe generation pipeline, enabling recipe personalization through user intervention.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/slacgismo\",\n      \"title\": \"SLAC GISMo · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Organization: SLAC GISMo (@slacgismo)\\n* Page: GitHub organization profile\\n* URL: https://github.com/slacgismo\\n* Location: SLAC National Accelerator Laboratory, Menlo Park, CA 94025\\n* Website: https://gismo.slac.stanford.edu/\\n* Top languages: Python, Jupyter Notebook, HTML\\nRepositories\\n* slacgismo/regrow\\n* slacgismo/ghrs-data: github-repository-stat data branch\\n* slacgismo/pv-validation-hub\\n* slacgismo/pv-validation-hub-client\\n* slacgismo/ascript: Advanced Smart Charting Infrastructure Planning Tool\\n* slacgismo/devine\\n* slacgismo/sg2t: SLAC GISMo Grid Tools\\n* slacgismo/recs_data: Residential Energy Consumption Survey data access\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07960\",\n      \"title\": \"[2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-02-15\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"archive\\nSearch Submit Donate Log in\\nComputer Science > Machine Learning\\narXiv:2302.07960 (cs)\\n[Submitted on 15 Feb 2023]\\nTitle: Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\\nAuthors: Bahare Fatemi , Quentin Duval , Rohit Girdhar , Michal Drozdzal , Adriana Romero-Soriano\\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\\nAbstract: Recipe personalization through ingredient substitution has the potential to help people meet their dietary needs and preferences, avoid potential allergens, and ease culinary exploration in everyone's kitchen.\\nTo address ingredient substitution, we build a benchmark, composed of a dataset of substitution pairs with standardized splits, evaluation metrics, and baselines.\\nWe further introduce Graph-based Ingredient Substitution Module (GISMo), a novel model that leverages the context of a recipe as well as generic ingredient relational information encoded within a graph to rank plausible substitutions.\\nWe show through comprehensive experimental validation that GISMo surpasses the best performing baseline by a large margin in terms of mean reciprocal rank. Finally, we highlight the benefits of GISMo by integrating it in an improved image-to-recipe generation pipeline, enabling recipe personalization through user intervention.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_eb4212b0affedKnHljTtyGboxL\"\n}"
